<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="761b624f">&lt;p&gt;
    Two basic approaches can be used to develop project milestones; one approach is to organize milestones around key
    deliverables, a second approach is to organize milestones around functional components.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Key points along the critical path are often referred to as &lt;strong&gt;milestones&lt;/strong&gt;. The main objective of
    establishing milestones is to provide a measurement of the project’s progress and success, and to allow early
    re-assessment of the ability to meet critical timelines. This measurement not only assists PMs in determining the
    project status but also provides critical management level information for assessing the successful completion of the
    project.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Project Milestones represent significant points within the project timeline from a stakeholder perspective. The Project
    Manager, Project Sponsor and key stakeholders should discuss what key milestones need to be identified. These could be
    the completion of major deliverables, dates when new compliance regulations take effect, or key decision making points.
    These designated Project Milestones will be highlighted, tracked and reported on in all key project
    communications.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Two approaches can be used to develop project milestones. One approach is to organize milestones around key
    deliverables. This approach works well for an assessment or design project, both of which tend to be more oriented
    toward written deliverables. A second approach is to organize milestones around functional components. This approach
    works well for an implementation project and identifies milestones based on the completion of or start of a group of
    related activities that are designed to deliver a chunk of functionality.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Milestones are internal or external to the project, and create high-level visibility and accountability. Thus, it is
    important to examine non-project factors that may impact a particular milestone. Capturing an external event(s) as a
    milestone will help others understand events outside of the Project Manager’s control.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    In summary, milestones mark a point of time for when at least one of the following is true:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Marks the completion of a group of related activities or deliverables
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is where key decisions or key deliverables must be completed for the project to advance
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is on the critical path for the project’s completion
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Can be tied to another project such that achievement of one of your project’s milestones may impact progress on
        other projects
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is agreed upon with the project team, key stakeholders and sponsors as a key point in the project
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is meaningful when communicating the status of a project with the project team, key stakeholders and sponsors
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.pm_small.base/guidances/guidelines/./resources/Milestones.png&quot; width=&quot;658&quot; height=&quot;336&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
